<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section>
  <title>Thursday, Jan 22</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      This is an outline of the topics we covered.
      You should not rely only on my notes.
      Make sure to take your own notes, and, if you miss a class, get notes from another student in class.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection xml:id="subsec-exact-equations">
    <title>Exact Equations</title>

    <p>
      We'll talk about just enough of the Calc III Chain Rule to motivate our next topic.
      Suppose <m>z = F(x, y)</m>.
      Then there are two partial derivatives <m>F_x, F_y</m>.
      But also, thinking of <m>y</m> as a function of <m>x</m>, there's a derivative <m>\frac{dy}{dx}</m>.
      If we start with an equation like <m>F(x, y) = C</m>, the Calc III Chain Rule gives:
      <md>
        <mrow> F_x + F_y \frac{dy}{dx} = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad M + N \frac{dy}{dx} = 0. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <p>
      So, suppose we start with a differential equation of the form:
      <md>
        <mrow> M(x, y) + N(x, y) \frac{dy}{dx} = 0. </mrow>
      </md>
      Then:
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Can we tell whether there is some <m>F(x, y)</m> such that <m>M = F_x, N = F_y</m>? If so, then the differential equation is called <term>exact</term>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            Can find find such an <m>F</m>?
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            If so, then the (implicit) solution to the differential equation is <m>F(x, y) = C</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </p>

    <fact>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For nice enough <m>F(x, y)</m>, <m>F_{xy} = F_{yx}</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
    </fact>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider <m>F(x, y) = xy + x^2 + \sin(y)</m>.
          Then:
          <md>
            <mrow> F_x \amp = y + 2x \amp F_{xy} \amp = 1 </mrow>
            <mrow> F_y \amp = x + \cos(y) \amp F_{yx} \amp = 1 </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      So, given <m>M + N \frac{dy}{dx} = 0</m>, the equation is exact if
      <md>
        <mrow> M_y = N_x. </mrow>
      </md>
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider <m>2x + y^2 + 2xy y' = 0</m>.
          Then:
          <md>
            <mrow> M_y = 2y = N_x \checkmark </mrow>
          </md>
          So the differential equation is exact.
          Now, let <m>F(x, y) = x^2 + xy^2</m>.
          Then:
          <md>
            <mrow> F_x \amp = 2x + y^2 = M \amp F_y \amp = 2xy = N </mrow>
          </md>
          So the solution to the differential equation is:
          <md>
            <mrow> x^2 + xy^2 = C. </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>

    <p>
      How did we find <m>F(x, y)</m>? We can play a sort of integral-derivative ping-pong.
      Once we know there exists <m>F</m> such that <m>F_x = M</m>, then:
      <md>
        <mrow> F = \int M\ dx = \int 2x + y^2\ dx = x^2 + xy^2 + C(y) </mrow>
      </md>
      Now, taking the partial derivative with respect to <m>y</m>, we should get <m>N</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow> 2xy + C'(y) \amp = 2xy \quad \Rightarrow \quad C'(y) = 0 \quad \Rightarrow \quad C(y) = k.</mrow>
      </md>
      So <m>F(x, y) = x^2 + 2xy + k</m>.
      (Note: we don't need to keep track of the <m>k</m>, since it can be absorbed into the <m>C</m> in the equation <m>F(x, y) = C</m>.)
    </p>

    <example>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Solve the initial value problem:
          <md>
            <mrow> \frac{dy}{dx} \amp = \frac{3 - 2xy^2}{2x^2y + 4}, \quad y(1) = 1 </mrow>
          </md>
          First, put the equation in a standard form:
          <md>
            <mrow> \underbrace{(2xy^2 - 3)}_{M} + \underbrace{(2x^2y + 4)}_{N}\frac{dy}{dx} = 0 </mrow>
            <mrow> M_y = 4xy = N_x \checkmark </mrow>
          </md>
          So the equation is exact.
          Then (an alternative to the ping-pong method):
          <md>
            <mrow> F \amp = \int 2xy^2 - 3\ dx = x^2y^2 - 3x </mrow>
            <mrow> F \amp = \int 2x^2y + 4\ dy = x^2y^2 + 4y </mrow>
          </md>
          Putting these together, <m>F(x, y) = x^2y^2 - 3x + 4y</m>.
          So the implicit solution is:
          <md>
            <mrow> x^2y^2 - 3x + 4y = C </mrow>
          </md>
          Applying the IC:
          <md>
            <mrow> 1 - 3 + 4 = C \quad \Rightarrow \quad C = 2 </mrow>
            <mrow> x^2y^2 - 3x + 4y = 2 </mrow>
          </md>
          We can find an explicit solution:
          <md>
            <mrow> x^2y^2 - 3x + 4y \amp = 2 </mrow>
            <mrow> x^2y^2 + 4y \amp = 2 + 3x </mrow>
            <mrow> y^2 + \frac{4}{x^2} y \amp = \frac{2}{x^2} + \frac{3}{x} </mrow>
            <mrow> \left(y + \frac{2}{x^2}\right)^2  - \frac{4}{x^4} \amp = \frac{2}{x^2} + \frac{3}{x} </mrow>
            <mrow> y + \frac{2}{x^2}  \amp = \pm \sqrt{\frac{2}{x^2} + \frac{3}{x} + \frac{4}{x^4}} </mrow>
            <mrow> y  \amp = \pm \sqrt{\frac{2}{x^2} + \frac{3}{x} + \frac{4}{x^4}} - \frac{2}{x^2} </mrow>
          </md>
          The IC decides the <m>\pm</m> sign:
          <md>
            <mrow> y  \amp = \sqrt{\frac{2}{x^2} + \frac{3}{x} + \frac{4}{x^4}} - \frac{2}{x^2} </mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </example>
  </subsection>
</section>